clean_summary:
Continuous language diffusion is emerging as a viable method for language modeling with very fast inference. Flow map language models (FMLMs) produce good results even with just a single forward pass.

@nmboffi: "Big update to our flow map language models paper! We believe this is the future of non-autoregressive text generation. We introduce a new class of continuous flow-based language models and distill them into their corresponding flow map for one-step text generation. We beat all discrete diffusion baselines at ~8x speed!"

The paper discusses how these models improve results across the board and showcase unique properties of continuous flows, such as straightforward inference-time steering and guidance. Techniques from continuous flows work incredibly well in practice for language generation, and the authors are excited about future developments in scaling, reasoning, and reinforcement learning-based fine-tuning.

Building Generative Image and Video models at scale has garnered a lot of interest recently. @sedielem provides a concise overview of the state of the art in generative image and video models, covering topics from modeling and architecture to distillation and control signals.

Sander Dieleman's background includes a storied career from early AlphaGo to leading research on Veo and @NanoBanana, as well as contributing to one of the most beloved AI blogs. This special talk was hosted in London, showcasing his insights.

@karpathy: "Common question: Can you train a language model with diffusion? My favorite answer: read this post (the whole blog is excellent)."

I've been talking a lot about the rehabilitation of continuous language diffusion recently, which seems to be in full swing. If you're curious to learn where things stand, read @FEijkelboom's blog post!

@FEijkelboom: "Flow-LLM Blogpost :D In the last few weeks, a bunch of work on flows for language came out. That is exciting, because it makes truly parallel text generation feel real: generation where models can keep refining the whole response during inference, instead of committing token by token. I wrote an intuitive and animated introduction to the area — why autoregression has a structural ceiling, why discrete diffusion only partly escapes it, and why flows may be the first genuinely parallel alternative. Here's an overview of the key parts of the blog - and let's chat at #ICLR2026 :)"

Flow-based language models aim to overcome the limitations of autoregressive models, enabling more efficient and parallel text generation.

Very nice blog post. Sander being Sander. My lens: amortization is about where you choose to close the loop. If inference-time integration is expensive but learnable, push it into training: learn the jump, bake in the trajectory, and turn an iterative loop into an amortized training-time fixed point.

But the dual is just as important: when training can’t compress the computation into weights, push it back to inference — adaptive computation, iterative refinement, verifier/search loops, test-time fixed points. So the interesting question isn’t 1-step vs iterative. It’s: which parts of the computation are worth amortizing, and which parts should remain online.

@sedielem: "My first blog post in over a year is a deep dive on flow maps, or how to learn the integral of a diffusion model to enable faster sampling and several other cool tricks. It's the longest one yet. Let me know what you think!"

